<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">ANZUS</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1951">1951</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Australia, New Zealand, United States Security Treaty</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">ANZUS is the security treaty signed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a> on 1 September 1951, each pledging to act against a common danger in the Pacific. It became the basis of Australian defence policy. After New Zealand barred nuclear-armed and nuclear-powered ships in the mid-1980s, the United States suspended its obligations to New Zealand in 1986, though the treaty remains in force between Australia and the United States.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anzus.jpg" alt="ANZUS" width="320" height="334" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Archives New Zealand from New Zealand · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ANZUS_Logo_(20921987801).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fall-of-singapore">fall of Singapore</a> and the decline of British power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> sought other partners for its security. Australia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> also feared a resurgent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and the spread of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a> to their north. In 1950 Percy Spender, Australia's minister for external affairs, sought a broad Pacific security agreement and said it would be meaningless without the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. The outbreak of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korean-war">Korean War</a> in mid-1950 changed American views. The treaty eased antipodean fears that Japan might again become a threat, though the United States was at first reluctant and the president offered an informal guarantee instead.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The treaty was concluded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a> on 1 September 1951 and entered into force on 29 April 1952. It recommits the parties to the purposes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>, and each declares that an armed attack on any of them would be dangerous to its own peace and safety and that it would act to meet the common danger in accordance with its constitutional processes. Unlike <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a>, it has no automatic commitment to armed assistance, since Spender expected the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-senate">United States Senate</a> to wish to keep the power to declare war. It also has no integrated defence structure or dedicated forces.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam-war">Vietnam War</a> directly as an ANZUS member. After the Labour Party won the New Zealand election in 1984, Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-lange">David Lange</a> barred nuclear-powered and nuclear-armed ships from New Zealand ports. In February 1985 New Zealand refused a visit by the destroyer USS Buchanan, because the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> would neither confirm nor deny whether its ships carried nuclear weapons.</p><p>After talks broke down, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> announced in 1986 that it was suspending its obligations to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>, calling it a friend but not an ally. Lange did not withdraw from the treaty, and New Zealand's policy became law in 1987. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> did not support the ban and continued to allow American ship visits. The treaty remains in force between Australia and the United States, whose officials meet annually in the AUSMIN talks.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “ANZUS” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>ANZUS Treaty</b> of 1951 made the American alliance the basis of Australian security.</blockquote><a href="/empire/commonwealth-of-australia">Commonwealth of Australia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>An artillery-based force, Kayforce, served in the Korean War from 1950, and in 1951 New Zealand signed the <b>ANZUS Treaty</b> with Australia and the United States, joining the South-East Asia Treaty Organization in 1954.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-zealand">New Zealand · Military</a></li></ul></section>
